    "q-213413": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213413",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${4}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,9=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative inverse of $4$ modulo $9,$ denoted ${4}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n4\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(4,9)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $9.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=9$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $4$ (modulo $9$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{2} & ≡4\\cdot 4≡16≡9+7≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ {4}^{3} & ≡4\\cdot 7≡28≡3\\cdot 9+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $4\\cdot 7≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $4$ modulo $9$ is $7.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
