    "q-213441": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213441",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${3}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,11=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $11,$ denoted ${3}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(3,11)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $11.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=11$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $3$ (modulo $11$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 9≡27≡2\\cdot 11+5≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot 5≡15≡11+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {3}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡11+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 4≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $11$ is $4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
