    "q-213437": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213437",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${3}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,7=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $7,$ denoted ${3}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n3\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(3,7)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $7.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=7$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $3$ (modulo $7$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2} & ≡3\\cdot 3≡9≡7+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ {3}^{3} & ≡3\\cdot 2≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ {3}^{4} & ≡3\\cdot 6≡18≡2\\cdot 7+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ {3}^{5} & ≡3\\cdot 4≡12≡7+5≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ {3}^{6} & ≡3\\cdot 5≡15≡2\\cdot 7+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $3\\cdot 5≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $3$ modulo $7$ is $5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
