    "q-213216": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213216",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${5}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,9=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative inverse of $5$ modulo $9,$ denoted ${5}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n5\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(5,9)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $9.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=9$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $5$ (modulo $9$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & ≡5\\cdot 5≡25≡2\\cdot 9+7≡7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ {5}^{3} & ≡5\\cdot 7≡35≡3\\cdot 9+8≡8 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ {5}^{4} & ≡5\\cdot 8≡40≡4\\cdot 9+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ {5}^{5} & ≡5\\cdot 4≡20≡2\\cdot 9+2≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ {5}^{6} & ≡5\\cdot 2≡10≡9+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $5\\cdot 2≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $5$ modulo $9$ is $2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
