    "q-213435": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213435",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Use trial and error to compute the following residue.\n\n${9}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,11=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The multiplicative inverse of $9$ modulo $11,$ denoted ${9}^{-1},$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n9\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).\n$$\n\nNote that since $\\text{gcd}(9,11)=1,$ an inverse element exists and is unique up to congruence modulo $11.$\n\nSince the modulus $n=11$ is small, we can find the inverse $x$ by computing exponents of $9$ (modulo $11$) until we get a result of $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2} & ≡9\\cdot 9≡81≡7\\cdot 11+4≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {9}^{3} & ≡9\\cdot 4≡36≡3\\cdot 11+3≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {9}^{4} & ≡9\\cdot 3≡27≡2\\cdot 11+5≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11) \\\\ {9}^{5} & ≡9\\cdot 5≡45≡4\\cdot 11+1≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $9\\cdot 5≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11).$ Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $9$ modulo $11$ is $5.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
          "tag": "mathquill",
