    "q-213513": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213513",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Fill in the blanks to make the following reasoning true.\n\nSince $\\text{gcd}(62,77)=$\n{{field-1}}, \nby the extended Euclidean algorithm, we have \n\n| $1\\,=\\,62\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,77\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-3}}\n$).$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, ${62}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,77=$\n{{field-4}} (expressed as the smallest nonnegative residue).",
      "worked_solution": "First of all, notice that $\\text{gcd}(62,77)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $62$ modulo $77$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $62$ modulo $77$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n62\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,77).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}77 & = & 62\\cdot 1 & + & 15 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 62 & = & 15\\cdot 4 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 2\\cdot 7 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =77-62 \\\\ 2 & =62-15\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & =15-2\\cdot 7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =15-2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =15-(62-15\\cdot 4)\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =15-62\\cdot 7+15\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 29-62\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =(77-62)\\cdot 29-62\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =77\\cdot 29-62\\cdot 29-62\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =62\\cdot (-36)+77\\cdot 29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $77,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}62\\cdot (-36)+77\\cdot 29 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,77) \\\\ 62\\cdot (-36) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,77) \\\\ 62\\cdot (77-36) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,77) \\\\ 62\\cdot 41 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,77)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $62\\cdot 41≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,77),$ which means that ${62}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,77=41.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
