    "q-213627": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213627",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Fill in the blanks to make the following reasoning true.\n\nSince $\\text{gcd}(189,281)=$\n{{field-1}}, \nby the extended Euclidean algorithm, we have \n\n| $1\\,=\\,189\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,281\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-3}}\n$).$ |\n| --- |\n\nTherefore, ${189}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,281=$\n{{field-4}} (expressed as the smallest nonnegative residue).",
      "worked_solution": "First of all, notice that $\\text{gcd}(189,281)=1.$ So, the multiplicative inverse of $189$ modulo $281$ exists.\n\nThe multiplicative inverse of $189$ modulo $281$ is the number $x$ such that\n\n$$\n189\\cdot x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,281).\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $x,$ we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}281 & = & 189\\cdot 1 & + & 92 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 189 & = & 92\\cdot 2 & + & 5 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 92 & = & 5\\cdot 18 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 5 & = & 2\\cdot 2 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}92 & =281-189 \\\\ 5 & =189-92\\cdot 2 \\\\ 2 & =92-5\\cdot 18 \\\\ 1 & =5-2\\cdot 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =5-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5-(92-5\\cdot 18)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\cdot 37-92\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(189-92\\cdot 2)\\cdot 37-92\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =189\\cdot 37-92\\cdot 76 \\\\  & =189\\cdot 37-(281-189)\\cdot 76 \\\\  & =189\\cdot 113-281\\cdot 76 \\\\  & =189\\cdot 113+281\\cdot (-76)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result modulo $281,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}189\\cdot 113+281\\cdot (-76) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,281) \\\\ 189\\cdot 113 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,281)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $189\\cdot 113≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,281),$ which means that ${189}^{-1}\\,\\text{mod}\\,281=113.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
